+# Payment-result guard validation
+
+## Scope and contribution
+
+This is a community/experimental result-consumption example, not a payment
+platform or production semantic verifier. CALL-E performs the call; the host owns
+binding, scheduling, persistence, permissions and execution. The checker answers
+whether supplied speech supports an extracted claim and which intermediate state
+is available. It does not establish identity or independently prove a payment.
+
+Direct users are community application developers; payment-follow-up operators
+benefit from explicit reasons and review boundaries. No measured time savings,
+recovery-rate change or reduction in real-world financial loss is claimed.
+
+## Baseline and reuse
+
+The inspected community baseline is `707122340774e3d63d5ce87c643695afc50d53cd`.
+kept's existing 160 tests passed before modification. Additional synthetic probes
+accepted `1k` as 100 minor units, conditional amounts as ordinary promises, and a
+negative quote paired with valid amount/date fields. These are controlled consumer
+inputs, not evidence that the live provider necessarily emits them.
+
+kept already provided binding, policy gates, SDK transport simulation, deduplication
+and ledger handling. Recover already provided webhook authentication, authoritative
+lookup and advisory-only/zero-recovery behavior. Those protections are reused,
+not counted as newly built. The new checks run before Promise construction and
+before retry-advice persistence, respectively.
+
+Verity's appointment gate, Rebuttal's question/answer checks, Kol's claim verifier,
+and Trunkline's quote/schema checks informed the design. Their domain engines are
+not runtime dependencies or claimed as generic payment validators. No upstream
+CALL-E SDK or authentication package is modified.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+Follow the setup in [the tool README](../../apps/python/payment-result-guard/README.md),
+then run from the repository root:
+
+```bash
+python apps/python/payment-result-guard/scripts/verify.py --output ../guard-report
+python3 scripts/validate_repository.py
+```
+
+The report distinguishes errors against manually assigned expectations from
+handling categories. A conditional/unsupported outcome is not automatically an
+error. Supported reported-paid statements still require external verification.
+The corpus has 42 explicit synthetic inputs: 26 promise and 16 recovery cases.
+Corpus counts are not the unit-test count; CLI, bridge faults and host regression
+tests add separate checks. Adapters replay the relevant subset through actual host
+shapes; host binding is additionally tested at its own boundary.
+
+## Evidence levels
+
+| Check | What executes | What is simulated |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Core regression | Pure evaluator and JSON CLI | All speech and extracted claims |
+| Adapter replay | kept and Recover mappings, same Python evaluator | Provider-shaped input records |
+| kept local runtime | Existing simulator, real installed SDK, capture, temporary ledger | CALL-E transport and business data |
+| Recover local runtime | Actual POST function, real Python subprocess, persistence assertions | Authentication decision/provider lookup and DB boundary |
+| Real integration | Not performed | No live CALL-E/Stripe or remote deployment evidence |
+
+Recover never creates a charge or reports recovered revenue. Its existing bounded
+no-answer follow-up remains subject to a bound terminal result and local controls;
+evidence failures do not become no-answer retries. The no-answer path requires one
+terminal attempt, its explicit destination matching the bound recipient, an empty
+string evidence field and an available array containing only valid bot turns (or
+no turns). Missing arrays are not treated as empty. Both adapters require an
+explicit matching attempt phone; missing or inconsistent destinations require review. Canceled records ignore late
+results. Duplicate-event handling remains; recovery after pre-registered events
+and failed re-fetch/DB work remains an existing limitation, not a solved feature.
+
+## Quality limits and intentional changes
+
+- Automatic acceptance supports only bounded en-US numeric USD and absolute-date
+  statements, plus explicit retry requests. Word numbers, relative dates and
+  unusual expressions require review. Normal fixtures were updated with explicit
+  independently written speech; old evidence-less inputs are retained as refusals.
+- A quoted substring cannot hide a condition in its complete source turn or a
+  supported later correction. Unknown speaker and multi-attempt ambiguity fail
+  closed. This still does not guarantee arbitrary natural-language understanding.
+- Arbitrary CLI labels such as `binding=matched` or `external_facts.source=stripe`
+  do not establish trust or authorize execution.
+- The Python bridge is a local Node deployment dependency. Missing executable,
+  timeout, bad JSON, overflow and protocol mismatch become review. Edge/serverless
+  deployment and packaging are not verified. Next build reports a dynamic executable
+  tracing warning; no deployment claim is made.
+- Recover's original full lint contains a pre-existing `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect`
+  error in `app/page.tsx`, plus two image warnings. This patch does not change that
+  page. Run targeted lint on changed files and distinguish it from full-project lint.
+- No provider credentials, real recordings or private financial records are used.
+  Test fetch/socket calls are disabled; existing fake transports supply all results.
+
+## AI-assisted implementation and human decisions
+
+AI assisted repository reading, reproduction, code changes, regression tests and
+this explanation. The user selected the payment-domain scope, two-case validation,
+intermediate-state boundary and local-only side effects. The implementation records
+red/green regressions for acceptance, bridge faults and route persistence. These
+synthetic checks are evidence of code behavior, not a measured business improvement.
+
+Next steps would require separate work: broader representative transcripts,
+operator review feedback, real-provider integration with explicit consent, and
+independent payment facts. None is required for this bounded contribution.
